WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:40.000
Welcome to OpenGit Ministry. This is a five-minute screenshot slideshow with generated narration, not a live interaction or an independent human rehearsal. Public-sector platform leaders need to discover reusable capabilities across repositories without confusing incomplete evidence with poor quality. The dashboard contains twelve demonstration records from a target cohort of one hundred. Scores, dates, candidate ministry assignments, and recommendations are illustrative. No live collection, database query, or AI request powers these screens. Consider one question: where might reuse deserve a conversation?

00:40.000 --> 01:30.000
Start with the portfolio overview. The numbers describe a demonstration snapshot, not a current inventory of government systems. The target cohort is not the completed analysis count. Look at evidence coverage before interpreting a health score. Missing information should reduce confidence rather than automatically penalize quality. A popular repository is not necessarily a supported service or the best reuse candidate. Ask which business decision matters and what sources would justify that decision. Pause here to review the visible fixture warning and identify an evidence gap.

01:30.000 --> 02:30.000
The candidate portfolio comparison separates activity and health from confidence and evidence coverage. Ministry and portfolio membership are hypotheses, not authoritative organizational records. An attention rule offers a question to investigate with a maintainer, not a judgment about a team. For a customer workshop, identify the owner who can confirm the grouping and explain the business purpose. Do not interpret similar scores as proof that services are interchangeable. Unknown documentation or testing evidence requires follow-up. Use the separate fictional workshop sample when named public examples are inappropriate. It is offline material, not data imported into this dashboard.

02:30.000 --> 03:30.000
The estate map places repositories alongside candidate ministries, portfolios, and capabilities. Its legend separates relationship meaning from provenance. Dashed inferred relationships must not be described as validated ownership. In the runnable dashboard you can pan and zoom; this recording shows a still image only. A shared capability label can justify comparing interfaces and constraints, but it cannot prove that implementations should be consolidated. Ask a platform owner and a service maintainer what evidence would confirm or reject one relationship. The proposed integrated architecture retains immutable commit references and evidence identifiers; that collection-to-dashboard path is not established by this fixture demonstration.

03:30.000 --> 04:30.000
Agentic opportunities keep value, readiness, and risk separate. These cards are illustrative recommendations, not live Foundry results and not permission to automate changes. A useful outcome is one bounded hypothesis for human review. For example, the fictional offline services both describe service discovery. Ask whether an interface could be reused before proposing consolidation. The evidence example includes a clearly fictional commit reference and an explicit limitation. Real pilot evidence must resolve to an approved immutable source, with tool versions, confidence, coverage, and redaction status. No source-organization write is authorized by this demo or its worksheet.

04:30.000 --> 05:00.000
Finish with the separate worksheet. Record the business question, evidence, uncertainty, owner role, and next action. It does not save entries; print to PDF before closing. Invite a platform lead and maintainer to a workshop. Agree on one question, approved repositories, acceptance criteria, and follow-up. Deployment, catalog approval, support ownership, and independent rehearsal remain release gates.
